<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jakob Erhardt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1823–1901</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Johann Jakob Erhardt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jakob Erhardt was a German missionary of the Church Missionary Society who worked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-krapf">Johann Krapf</a> on the East African coast from 1849. He visited the Shambaa kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimweri-ye-nyumbai">Kimweri ye Nyumbai</a> and studied local languages. In 1856 he drew a map of a great inland sea based on caravan reports, which helped inspire Burton and Speke's expedition to the lakes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jakob-erhardt.jpg" alt="Jakob Erhardt" width="320" height="484" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: n.n. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann-jakob-ehrhardt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
